WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.880
As many Cleveland sports fans will remember,

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:12.480
NBA superstar LeBron James took his talents to South Beach in 2010,

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:14.860
only to return to Cleveland in 2014.

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:17.360
In a new paper, Cleveland-born Danny Shoag and I

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:21.160
look at what LeBron's moves between Miami and Cleveland

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:23.200
meant for the economies of each city.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:26.180
We looked at the number of bars and restaurants within a mile

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:28.340
of the American Airlines Arena in Miami

00:00:28.349 --> 00:00:30.269
and the Quicken Loans Arena in Cleveland,

00:00:30.269 --> 00:00:33.960
and then compared years in which LeBron was present to years in which he was not.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:38.100
Now, when LeBron is around, there are 13 percent more bars and restaurants,

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.440
and they employ a little under 25 percent more people.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:44.120
To ensure that this is indeed a "LeBron effect",

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:47.360
and not simply the results of citywide booms and busts,

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:50.500
we check what happens farther out: not much, it turns out.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:52.380
This confirms our suspicion:

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:56.000
that sports superstars can indeed have a noticeable economic impact,

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.260
but that that impact is very local.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:01.140
These effects are greater in Cleveland than they are in Miami -

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:05.460
and that is a clear indication that LeBron should not have left in the first place.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:09.400
To read our full paper on the "LeBron effect",

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:12.180
check the links in the description below.

